WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:12.240
Back in the early 1970s, Stephen Hawking wrote
down an astonishing equation. It would include

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:17.039
relativity. It would include quantum mechanics
and it would include information.

00:00:17.039 --> 00:00:24.959
Hawking’s rather simple equation brought us a step closer to understanding the relationship between quantum physics and black holes.

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:34.360
When an object crosses over the edge of a
black hole, its event horizon, the object

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:39.610
enters basically a realm of empty space and
darkness and it continues to be dragged towards

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:44.489
the center of the black hole toward what we
call the singularity where it gets crushed

00:00:44.489 --> 00:00:50.260
out of existence. Every object, in some sense,
contains information because it contains a

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:55.879
very specific arrangement of particles. So
where is the information that describes the

00:00:55.879 --> 00:00:59.599
arrangement of those particles? Where does it go?

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:10.229
Hawking’s description of this process was that the energy remains but the information
disappears. For many years - for decades - people

00:01:10.229 --> 00:01:17.040
wondered, ‘Is Hawking right?’ Is the information
obliterated - it disappears from the universe

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:24.439
- or is it still there, and perhaps can in
some way retrieved? The destruction of information

00:01:24.439 --> 00:01:28.549
was counter-intuitive and it didn’t match
the rest of things we knew. In all parts of

00:01:28.549 --> 00:01:34.149
physics we had a situation where information
doesn’t get destroyed. So it was a bit puzzling.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:43.829
This debate furiously went back and forth
up through the 80s and into the 90s, when

00:01:43.829 --> 00:01:50.930
people finally began to articulate this new
principle, this holographic principle. And

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:57.380
what it said is that all the things that were
falling inside a black hole were somehow captured

00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:04.979
in a preserved image at the horizon itself.
So if the information is not lost on the surface,

00:02:04.979 --> 00:02:10.020
it is not lost inside because they are equivalent.
All of the information about those objects

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:15.300
- what they were like in their three-dimensional
existence - was preserved or encoded on the

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:21.230
surface of the black hole. And that’s a
little bit like a hologram. Well that suggests

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:27.819
that maybe that idea may apply more broadly
to the universe as a whole. Maybe the three-dimensional

00:02:27.819 --> 00:02:32.660
objects - us, everything in the world around
us - maybe all of the information in these

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:38.459
objects is carried, is smeared around a distant
two-dimensional surface that surrounds us.

00:02:38.459 --> 00:02:46.140
And we’re just, in some sense, a holographic
projection of that distant data.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:52.950
The holographic principle tells us something quite astonishing. It says that our ideas of volume, the real

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:56.129
world in a sense, might be a kind of illusion.

